{
  "primitive": "bigint",
  "source": "Knuth TAoCP Vol 2 \u00a74.3 multiple-precision arithmetic identities. HeavyThing bigint performs standard signed integer arithmetic; magnitude is byte-aligned big-endian hex (even-length, leading zero nibble preserved on the top byte, '00' for zero, per bigint$encode). Sign is encoded out-of-band via the optional boolean fields a_negative / b_negative / expected_negative (absent == false), so every a_hex / b_hex / expected_hex / remainder_hex field is strictly even-length lowercase magnitude hex with no sign or separator. op 'div' returns the quotient in expected_hex with the matching remainder in remainder_hex (dividend == divisor*quotient + remainder, per bigint$divide). isprime / isprime2 return 01 (prime) or 00 (composite). All values cross-checked against Python integer arithmetic and captured from the HeavyThing reference build (bigint$add / subtract / multiply / divide / modby / inversemod / isprime / isprime2).",
